.TH TANGSIBLE 1 "August 2026" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.SH NAME
tangsible \- a live terminal UI for ansible\-playbook
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tangsible
.I verb
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tangsible
wraps
.BR ansible-playbook (1)
in a live, navigable tree of plays \(-> tasks \(-> hosts, color\-coded by
outcome (OK / Changed / Skipped / Failed / Unreachable). It comes up
immediately and updates live while the playbook is still running, and lets
you drill into any host's result \(em task source, output, stderr,
.B \-\-diff
output, full JSON \(em without losing your place in the run.
.PP
It is a development and troubleshooting interface for
.BR ansible-playbook ,
not a replacement for it or an orchestration platform. Tangsible is a
single static binary that shells out to
.B ansible\-playbook
exactly as you would yourself, and turns its output into something you can
watch and navigate as it happens. It is aimed at someone running playbooks
by hand, repeatedly, against a live-ish environment \(em not at CI
pipelines, scheduled automation, or fleet-scale inventories.
.PP
Every
.I verb
below is documented in its own manual page. This page covers what all of
them have in common: configuration, environment variables, and files.
.SH VERBS
.TP
.BR tangsible-run (1)
Run a playbook and show its live progress in the tree UI. The everyday
entry point.
.TP
.BR tangsible-rerun (1)
Re\-open the rerun dialog directly from the command line, pre\-filled from
the most recent invocation.
.TP
.BR tangsible-role (1)
Run a single role in isolation, without hand\-writing a wrapper playbook.
.TP
.BR tangsible-template (1)
Render one Jinja2 template through Ansible's own templating engine,
against a real inventory host.
.TP
.BR tangsible-host (1)
Show everything Tangsible can determine about one host: live facts,
inventory group membership, which plays would run, host_vars, and the raw
.B ansible\-inventory \-\-host
dump.
.TP
.BR tangsible-hosts (1)
Browse every inventory host, then drill into any one of them with the same
view
.B tangsible host
shows.
.TP
.BR tangsible-revisit (1)
Browse previous runs and reopen one, exactly as it looked when it
finished.
.TP
.BR tangsible-vault (1)
Edit individually vault\-encrypted variables in a file in place, without
hand\-running
.B "ansible\-vault encrypt_string"
and pasting ciphertext back yourself.
.TP
.B tangsible version
Print the version, git commit, build date, and Go toolchain, then exit.
Opens no UI and reads no inventory.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B tangsible
requires
.B ansible\-playbook
and the
.B ansible.posix
collection to be installed and on
.BR PATH :
.RS
.EX
ansible\-galaxy collection install ansible.posix
.EE
.RE
.PP
That collection provides the
.B jsonl
stdout callback plugin Tangsible relies on to receive events while the
playbook runs. Because it opens a full\-screen terminal UI, Tangsible
requires a real TTY and cannot run in a piped or headless shell.
.SH CONFIGURATION
When a
.I verb
that needs a playbook is not given one explicitly, Tangsible resolves one
in order from:
.IP 1. 4
.B $TANGSIBLE_PLAYBOOK
.IP 2. 4
.B .tangsible/config.toml
in the current directory
.IP 3. 4
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tangsible/config.toml
(default
.BR ~/.config/tangsible/config.toml )
.IP 4. 4
.B ./site.yml ,
if present
.PP
Both config files share the same TOML shape:
.RS
.EX
[general]
default_playbook = "myplaybook.yml"
default_tree_state = "collapsed"   # "expanded" or "collapsed"
two_pane_layout = true             # default: true
color = true                       # default: true

[vault]
password_file = "./.vault_pass"    # tangsible\-vault(1)'s own fallback
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B default_tree_state
and the global
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
file's own
.B default_tree_state
are only ever read from the project\-local
.B .tangsible/config.toml ,
never the global one.
.B two_pane_layout
governs whether the drill\-down view keeps the tree pane visible
alongside it on a wide enough terminal.
.B color
governs whether a task row's collapsed per\-host list may render in
color at all, independent of terminal capability and
.BR NO_COLOR .
.B "[vault] password_file"
is likewise only ever read from the project\-local file; see
.BR tangsible-vault (1)
for its full precedence order.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .tangsible/config.toml
Project\-local, user\-authored settings, as above. Never written by
Tangsible itself \(em safe to hand\-edit, including comments.
.TP
.B .tangsible/state.toml
App\-owned invocation history and last\-run state, backing
.BR tangsible-rerun (1)
and
.BR tangsible-revisit (1).
Rewritten on every invocation of every verb; contains hostnames, tags,
and extra\-vars from past invocations, so it should be listed in
.BR .gitignore .
.TP
.B .tangsible/runs/<run\-id>.jsonl
.TQ
.B .tangsible/runs/<run\-id>.stderr
One generation's raw saved event stream and stderr, kept for as long as
its
.B .tangsible/state.toml
history entry references it. Backs
.BR tangsible-revisit (1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TANGSIBLE_PLAYBOOK
Highest\-priority source for an unspecified playbook path; see
CONFIGURATION above.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the global config file; see CONFIGURATION above.
.TP
.B VISUAL, EDITOR
Consulted in that order by
.BR tangsible-template (1)'s
.B e
key, and by
.BR tangsible-vault (1)
to open its editable view. Falls back to
.B vi
if neither is set.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set to any value (including empty), disables color in the tree's
collapsed per\-host summary row, per the
.UR https://no-color.org
no\-color.org
.UE
convention.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The playbook completed successfully; completed with only unreachable
hosts and no other failure; or the run was interrupted by the user
.RB ( q
or
.BR Ctrl-C ).
None of these are treated as a Tangsible failure.
.TP
.B 1
The playbook genuinely failed (any exit code other than the two benign
cases above), or the terminal UI itself errored.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: a required argument was missing, or no playbook could be
resolved.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B strategy: free
is not supported \(em Tangsible's data model assumes hosts progress
through tasks together, which
.B free
deliberately violates; the tree stays empty for such a run even though
the playbook itself runs and finishes normally underneath.
Interactive input requested
.I after
the TUI has taken over the terminal is not supported (a bare
.B pause:
task with no
.B seconds:
does not wait;
.B \-\-step
crashes outright).
.B \-\-ask\-become\-pass
and
.B \-\-ask\-vault\-pass
work normally, since Ansible prompts for them before Tangsible ever
takes over the terminal (unrelated to
.BR tangsible-vault (1)'s
own, same\-named
.B \-\-ask\-vault\-pass
flag, which is Tangsible's own prompt, not a passthrough to Ansible's).
Tangsible is developed and tested against inventories on the order of
ten hosts, not fleet scale.
.SH SEE ALSO
